> The stage lights were blinding my eyes, the speakers were deafening my
> ears, but that's OK, for I have seen and heard all I need in life.

> This concert was like a good dream: You can only experience it once, and you
> are unable to vividly replay it from memory. However, you know that it was
> good––so good that you want to experience it again, yet you know you can't.

> I regret going to this concert. Music will never sound this good to me ever again.

These were my attempts at summarizing my experience at the concert down to _quotes_.
I hope I got my point across. It was amazing beyond words. In fact, me
describing it doesn't do this concert justice. You should find a Higuchi Ai
concert near you and book a ticket immediately.

For clarity, her name is Ai and Higuchi is her family name. I refer to her
as Higuchi Ai because that's the ordering she uses: [ヒグチアイ (HIGUCHIAI)](https://www.higuchiai.com/).

If you're just interested about the concert itself, skip to [this section](#concert-itself).

# Before the concert

To be very honest, I wasn't gonna attend this concert the first time I found out
about it. For the longest time, I had always thought that I don't enjoy concerts
(or live music). They're loud and contain a big crowd––two things I strongly
dislike. So, when I first got an email about this one, I just shrugged it off as
another failed attempt by the tracked ads algorithm. However, two things
happened that led to me changing my mind.

First was [Natsu Go Cloud](https://www.facebook.com/share/p/14t653Hz7iL/),
an anime convention with a night party that had a "concert." Specifically, the
organizers call it a "Live House After Party Stage" where there were two DJs and
a rock band who took turns playing J-pop and anime music throughout the night.
That was the first time in recent memory where I thought, "Yea, maybe I DO like
concerts." Looking back, there is one very clear difference between this one and
the few ones I went to before this: The previous ones I went to didn't play the
music I wanted to listen to.

For context, the last concert I went to was a multi-artist concert held in Mahidol
University from around 2024. I can't remember the specific bands that performed but
I can tell you that I wouldn't have gone if my friends didn't invite me as it was the
last big thing we could do with each other in college before graduating.
Sometimes, the people performing matter less compared to the people you were
going with.

I didn't enjoyed it much (and in fact, walked out mid-way), so that made me
believe that concerts weren't for me. I was wrong, and the concert(s) at 
Natsu Go Cloud changed my mind.

So, that's one hurdle cleared: I do like concerts. But why Higuchi Ai?

Simple answer: [Her THE FIRST TAKE performance of Akuma no Ko](https://youtu.be/bOLYBRRpRsU?si=GvL4_gLv7G7Ng0sJ).
For those who are unaware, THE FIRST TAKE is a YouTube channel that invites
artists to come perform their popular songs. Their gimmick is that there is only
one take. Of course, it is impossible for us viewers to truly verify that.
However, I know that these artists do perform live and they are fully capable
of dishing out performances like that. So, I do trust that these videos are
truly from _the first take_.

Back to why it made me want to attend _Higuchi Ai's concert_. This video got
recommended to me (good job, YouTube) and it made me think:
"If she sounds this good with YouTube compression, imagine how good she sounds
live?" From there, I started wishing that I had listened to her live. Then, I
was once again reminded about the concert from a coworker who sent me a post on
Instagram about it. To go or not to go, there is one clear answer.

So, I bought the ticket.

# [The concert itself]($heading.id('concert-itself'))

The concert was held on 23 October 2026 at the The Street Hall, The Street
Ratchada. The doors were scheduled to open at 6PM sharp but I was having dinner
and finishing up some urgent work before, so I actually went into the concert
hall at around 6:40PM. I had bought the VIP ticket, so it meant that I got a seat
very close to the stage. The seats were first-come-first-served, so I was sat
way to the side. However, it did give me a very good view of Higuchi Ai herself.

Once the clock hit 7PM, we saw silhouettes of performers coming up on stage. You
could tell which one was Higuchi Ai even without the spotlight because she was
wearing a bright red dress. No one would be able to miss that even in the dark.
After some final sound check, the music started. Finally, the concert has truly
begun. Following is my recollection of the concert to the best of my memory.

We began with an absolute banger: [What now burns in my chest – 今この胸に滾るのは](https://youtu.be/4q-MBRA2D7w)
It was the ending theme for the anime Snowball Earth and it starts with a drum
and piano combo. I remember almost falling of my seats because the music just
started so loudly and suddenly that I was not prepared for it. Of course, I
didn't just buy a ticket just to listen to the instruments. I was here to listen
to Higuchi Ai sing. Obviously, I was blown away. Whatever expectectation I had
from that THE FIRST TAKE video, she absolutely blew pass it. Her voice was very
powerful. Even if I don't fully understand the lyrics of the song, I can feel the strong
emotions coming through just from her voice and her keyboard. It's astonishing
that there are people who can play an instrument and sing at the same time. I
know they exist, but I'm still amazed every time I see and hear one. I have
nothing to say but that this was an intense start to the concert.

Right after the first song ended, the next one started (as the audience were
cheering). [Triangle – トライアングル](https://youtu.be/pJi5eQaowZI) is our next
song and it was the ending theme for the second season of the anime Ao Ashi.
Again, an absolute banger. This song has parts that require a higher pitch than
the previous song so, apart from her strong alto singing, we the audience were
also blessed with the wonderful high notes that were just perfect. Honestly,
words don't do her voice justice, so I'm gonna stop the repetitive glazing from
now on.

After Triangle ended, we finally got some audience engagement. Higuchi Ai
introduced herself to the audience and asked us to call her Ai-chan (I will
refer to her as this for the rest of this blog) and had a small talk about the
audience's favorite animes. The obvious answer is Attack on Titan. Akuma no
Ko––the name of the concert––is the ending theme of a season of Attack on Titan,
so it makes a lot of sense that many of the audience members are Attack on Titan
fans. In fact, there were a few cosplayers at the concert. One was dressed up as
Mikasa in her casual outfit, another was cosplaying as a member of the Survey
Corps, and many people just had a red scarf with them––presumably as an homage
to Mikasa. Other answers included Snowball Earth and Ao Ashi, two animes that
Ai-chan has sung the ending themes for. An interesting answer was Bocchi The
Rock! Yes, it is a popular series, but I didn't know that Ai-chan had anything
to do with it. That was when I found out that she wrote the lyrics for some of
the songs that were in the anime. This transitions perfectly into the next few
songs she sang. I really do wonder if some of the audience were asked by the
staff to answer certain questions a certain way to make these smooth transitions
into the upcoming songs.

At this point, the other band members had left the stage, leaving just Ai-chan
alone with her keyboard. Then, she played the songs from Bocchi the Rock! that
she had written the lyrics for:
[Seishun Complex – 青春コンプレックス (Kessoku Band)](https://youtu.be/7FDRQifEMUQ)
(the opening theme) and
[If I could be a constellation – 正座になれたら (Kessoku Band)](https://youtu.be/-LwBbLa_Vhc)
(an insert theme). Even if it's just her and her keyboard, the performance
didn't feel lacking at all. It still was one of the most wonderful musical
performances I'd witnessed.

The next song was a surprise:
[Why don't you stay – แค่เธอ (Jeff Satur)](https://youtu.be/RVv_rate258). I am
quite familiar with this song because I used to listen to it a lot a few years ago.
However, I would––never in a million years––expect it to show up at
a Higuchi Ai concert. She still performed it perfectly, almost as if she had
become Jeff Satur but female right then and there. (OK, I'll stop the glazing.)

Next up were some of Ai-chan's original songs. The band members had come back on
stage, and we got to listen to [Gekijyou – 劇場](https://youtu.be/mPZ-5I1bAIY) live.
I'm sure there were a few more songs like
[Kanashii Uta ga Aru Riyuu – 悲しい歌がある理由](https://youtu.be/sPge9xBPej8)
but honestly, I stopped trying to figure out which song it was and just enjoyed
the performance. I mean, I paid good money to be here.

Up next was a Q&A session––a first for concerts apparently. Ai-chan herself
calls it "Talk time," which I tend to agree. The questions asked were collected
from the audience. Specifically, sending in a question was a privilege that only
the VVIP audience members had, but at least everyone got to hear the answer.
There were quite a few questions but here are my favorites.

- Q: What is your favorite color?
  - A: (pointing to her entirely red dress) Red
- Q: What is your impression of Thailand?
  - A: It's hot, it's humid, and it's hard to breath in. But I love Thai food,
    though. I eat it once a week. I had Moo Krob, Leng Zaap, and Khao Nhiew
    Mamuang since arriving in Thailand. I want to eat Poo Phad Phong Curry after
    this.
- Q: Which song describes your life right now?
  - Gekijyou, because life is about the people that come and go in your life.
    Everything that you have experience in life becomes a part of who you are,
    just like how the Thai food I ate became a part of me.

These aren't exact quotes––they are paraphrases.

Another funny thing that happened during the Q&A was the band member
introduction. Technically, Ai-chan is just a singer/keyboardist/song writer. So,
for concerts, she brings in people to play the other instruments in the band.
That's not the funny part, though. The funny part is, as she introduced each
member, she asked them to say what their favorite Thai food is. The guitarist
liked Phak Boong Mhoo Sab (ผักบุ้งหมูสับ), the drummer likes Tom Yum Kung (ต้มยำกุ้ง),
and the bassist liks Pad Thai (ผัดไทย). Ai-chan herself loves Puu Phad Phong
Curry (ปูผัดผงกะหรี่), and plans to have it after the concert. A cultured
preference, I must say. I think that dish is very delicious as well. It's just
not very common because crab meat is quite expensive and not something you eat
very often as a person living in Bangkok.

After the Q&A session, we reached the main event. The reason everyone came here
for: The Attack on Titan endings. Ai-chan said that, if it wasn't for her
involvement with Attack on Titan, she wouldn't be in Thailand right now. I'm
glad she got involved, because no one in the audience would have been blessed by
her performance otherwise.

The next two songs were [Itterashai –
いってらっしゃい](https://youtu.be/LE2_BeKcG4E) (ending theme of Attack on Titan: The
Final Season - Kanketsu-hen) and [Akuma no Ko – 悪魔の子](https://youtu.be/WPl10ZrhCtk)
(ending theme of Attack on Titan: The Final Season Part 2). I seriously am at a
loss for words trying to describe these two songs' performance. In short, you could really
feel the emotions behind these two melancholic songs. To me, these two songs
represent Mikasa and Eren, respectively. Itterashai is about saying one last
good bye to your most beloved one and wondering about the what-ifs of your life
together. Akuma no Ko, on the other hand, is about sacrificing everything in
this cruel world for the sake of your loved ones. I'm sure you know how hard
these hit if you've finished Attack on Titan. Even if you're not good at
Japanese or don't know the language at all, I'm confident that you'd be able to
feel these emotions behind these songs as well if you listened to Ai-chan
performing them.

After these two songs ended, I genuinely thought that my life had genuinely
reached its peak. Nothing in life could ever top this. These were probably
the remaining songs. They were amazing still, fitting for the ending act of
this spectacular concert.

- [Yukari – 縁](https://youtu.be/R-lLH9RDFm4)
- [Massarana Daichi – まっさらな大地](https://youtu.be/fMgPOhjdUOQ)
- [Yamerunara Ima – やめるなら今](https://youtu.be/6maqCmfEmT8)

There are probably more, but this is about as much as I remember. I think your
brain just shut down once it realizes that it will never reach a high like
this ever again. If the actual set list is ever released, I will update it here.

Since I bought the VIP ticket, I was entitled to a meet & greet session. What
this session really is is just a photo op with Ai-chan and getting a handshake
with her. I think many people will find it hard to justify the price hike of
around 1,500 baht over the regular ticket just for this, the seats closer to
the stage, and a signed poster. Personally, I have mixed feelings about paying
more for I am a very stingy person, but I honestly don't regret buying the VIP
ticket. Even if I feel like the extra goods aren't worth the price difference,
I'm still happy to have paid for this concert just for the eargasm it gave me.

# After the concert

Honestly, I still haven't fully processed the concert. It was great beyond
words. I wish I could go back in time to experience it again because no
recording will be able to provide me with the same feelings I experienced during
the event. I genuinely regret going because no music will be able to make me
experience the same level of euphoria I did during the concert itself. I think
my only way out is just to wait for another Higuchi Ai concert and getting a
ticket for it. I'm sure that's not healthy for my finances. But if it was
for Higuchi Ai's concert, maybe any amount of money paid is worth. That's just
how amazing of an artist she is.

# Higuchi Ai and Attack on Titan

This part isn't related to the concert directly, I just felt like sharing
because it was on my mind as I was commuting back from the concert.

Going back to this sentence: _"Ai-chan said that, if it wasn't for her
involvement with Attack on Titan, she wouldn't be in Thailand right now."_ I do
see where she was coming from. I wouldn't have known about Higuchi Ai if it wasn't
for Attack on Titan. I have watched Bocchi the Rock! before, but I didn't really
go into the weeds to see who wrote the lyrics when I first saw it. Although _If
I could be a constellation_ is my favorite BTR song, I wouldn't have
discovered Higuchi Ai back then.

It's not that I think that her involvement with Attack on Titan is a bad thing,
but it does make me wonder if she would've achieved the same level of popularity
if she wasn't involved. Not for a lack of skill, but a lack of luck. Her
capabilities certainly wouldn't be different. Singing, song writing, playing the
piano/keyboard, that doesn't come from being part of a popular anime. However, I have
a feeling that significantly less people would've found out about her without
Attack on Titan. You might get a YouTube recommendation of her music, but that's
not the same level of exposure as being part of a popular work that is known
throughout the world. And if you're also already talented, then that exposure
might just change your life in ways you can't even begin to imagine.

Really, it just makes me wonder how many artists there are who are as good but
no one knows about because they weren't lucky enough to get this same level of exposure.

Another thing is that Higuchi Ai is a Japanese artist. Japan doesn't have good track
record when it comes to exporting their talents to the rest of the world. You
might know about Yoasobi, Ado, Kenshi Yonezu, Fujii Kaze, but I would say that
they are not as well-known as the artists from the neighboring country of
South Korea. If you walk up to a random person, my bet is that they're more
likely to know about BTS or Black Pink than about the previously mentioned
popular Japanese artists. It also makes me kinda sad to know that it's less
likely to get Japanese artists to hold concerts in Thailand compared to artists
from elsewhere.

At the end of the day, I'm glad that she wrote and sang the two ending themes
for Attack on Titan because it led her to hold this concert. It was a
life-changing experience for me (and probably the rest of the audience, too),
and I'm thankful for everything she has done in her life that led up to this.

Thank you, Attack on Titan and the organizers in Thailand for making this happen.

On an unrelated note, this just goes to show that you DO need luck to succeed.
However, luck alone also wouldn't be enough. Luck and skills go hand-in-hand.
I think that if Higuchi Ai's other songs weren't good, then there would've
been less people attending the concerts. Paying thousands of Baht just to listen
to 2 songs just doesn't make sense to me.

# Closing thoughts

I'm glad I went. I'm sad I can't experience it again. Cheers.

